---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — 오래 남는 것과 세게 꽂히는 것. (/staying-power)
 *
 * ── 왜 이 지면인가 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `/watched` 는 같은 자료를 **시청시간 순 하나**로만 세운다. 그런데 이 자료에는
 * **주수(얼마나 오래)**와 **주당 시청시간(얼마나 세게)**이 같이 들어 있고, 그 둘은 다른 축이다.
 * 상위 8을 둘로 뽑으면 겹치는 것이 **두 편뿐**이다 — 한 줄로 세울 수 없다는 증거다.
 *
 * ── 기사와 서로 검산한다 (2번이 알려 준 순서) ──────────────────
 * `/article/netflix-korean-catalogue-concentration` 이 같은 자료로 이미 나갔다.
 * 거기 적은 「1위는 시간을 2.2배로 만들지만 주수는 0.1주 차이」가 이 지면의 숫자와 맞아야 한다.
 *
 * ── ⚠ 상위 50편이다. 294편 전체가 아니다 ─────────────────────
 * 넷플릭스가 주간 10위까지만 낸다. 화면에 **몇 편 중 몇 편인지** 항상 박는다.
 *
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production 승인 전이다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-staying-power.json';
import KcwMethodLimits from '../../components/KcwMethodLimits.astro';

const TITLE = 'Staying power and force — two different things';
const DESC =
  'Korean titles on Netflix’s global chart, measured two ways: how many weeks they lasted and how '
  + 'many hours they drew per week. Of the top eight on each measure, only two appear on both.';

const M = (v: number) => (v / 1_000_000).toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 }) + 'm';
const bn = (v: number) => (v / 1_000_000_000).toFixed(2) + 'bn';
const 겹침 = data.longest.filter((a) => data.fiercest.some((b) => b.title === a.title)).map((a) => a.title);
const p1 = data.peakGroups[0];
const pn = data.peakGroups[1];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd} canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/staying-power">
  <h1>Staying power and force are two different things</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The {data.shown} biggest Korean titles on Netflix’s global weekly chart, out of {data.titleCount}
    that have appeared since {data.weekFrom}. Ranked once by how long they lasted and once by how
    hard they landed — the two lists are almost different lists.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>The same {data.shown} titles, ordered two ways</h2>
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <h3>Lasted longest</h3>
        <ol>
          {data.longest.map((r) => (
            <li>
              <span class="nm">{r.title}</span>
              <span class="meta"><b>{r.weeks}</b> weeks · {M(r.perWeek)}/week</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Landed hardest</h3>
        <ol>
          {data.fiercest.map((r) => (
            <li>
              <span class="nm">{r.title}</span>
              <span class="meta"><b>{M(r.perWeek)}</b>/week · {r.weeks} weeks</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Of the eight on each side, <b>{겹침.length}</b> appear on both — {겹침.join(' and ')}.
      A title that runs for months and a title that consumes a fortnight are both “hits”, and the
      single number usually quoted for either one cannot tell them apart.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/*
    🔴 2026-08-08 정정. 여기 제목이 「Reaching number one doubles the hours. It adds no weeks.」였다.
       그 수는 **시간 상위 50편 안에서** 낸 것이다 — 시간으로 고른 50편을 놓고 시간을 설명했다.
       235편 전부로 재면 주수가 갈린다(중앙 5주 대 2주). 「주수는 그대로」는 상위 50편이
       다들 9주쯤이라 그렇게 보였을 뿐이다. 무리를 전체로 다시 세고 제목을 고쳤다.
    ⛔ 평균만 내지 않는다. 한 편이 5.05bn 을 가져가는 분포라 평균은 그 한 편을 말한다.
  */}
  <section>
    <h2>Reaching number one goes with longer runs, not just bigger weeks</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Group</th><th>Titles</th><th>Median hours</th><th>Median weeks</th><th>Avg hours</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.peakGroups.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{g.label}</td>
              <td>{g.n}</td><td class="tot">{M(g.medianHours)}</td>
              <td class="tot">{g.medianWeeks}</td><td>{M(g.avgHours)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      All {data.titleCount} titles, not the fifty largest. The median title that reached number one
      drew <b>{(p1.medianHours / pn.medianHours).toFixed(1)}×</b> the hours of one that did not, and
      it also ran <b>{p1.medianWeeks} weeks against {pn.medianWeeks}</b>. Most of that gap is length,
      not landing: hold the run length fixed and what remains is{' '}
      <b>{data.weeksBands.find((b) => b.label === '6–10 weeks')?.ratio}×</b> among titles that lasted
      six to ten weeks.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Titles that lasted</th><th>Reached #1</th><th>Median hours</th><th>Did not</th><th>Median hours</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.weeksBands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.label}</td>
              <td>{b.topN}</td><td class="tot">{b.topN ? M(b.topMedianHours) : '—'}</td>
              <td>{b.restN}</td><td class="tot">{b.restN ? M(b.restMedianHours) : '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Neither number is the cause of the other.</b> Weeks on chart and peak rank track total hours
      about equally well ({data.correlations.weeksVsLogHours} and{' '}
      {Math.abs(data.correlations.peakVsLogHours)} against log hours), and they track each other at{' '}
      {Math.abs(data.correlations.weeksVsPeak)}, so a table that shows one is partly showing the other.
      Reaching number two goes with a higher median than reaching number one
      ({M(data.peakByRank[1].medianHours)} against {M(data.peakByRank[0].medianHours)}) — the order
      does not run down the ranks.
      {' '}<a href="/article/reaching-number-one-is-mostly-length">The full story →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  {/*
    ⚠ 2026-08-08. 위 정정을 내면서 **한 주짜리가 얼마나 많은지**를 세어 놓고 안 보여 주고 있었다.
      세어 놓고 안 내면 안 센 것과 같다 — `check-data-keys-shown.mjs` 가 그것을 잡았다.
    ⛔ 줄세우기가 아니다. 「가장 흔한 결과가 무엇인가」다.
  */}
  <section>
    <h2>The most common outcome is one week</h2>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{data.oneWeekOnly} of {data.titleCount}</b> Korean titles that reached a global Non-English
      Top 10 — <b>{((100 * data.oneWeekOnly) / data.titleCount).toFixed(1)}%</b> — were on it for
      exactly one week. Half the panel was gone within two.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Weeks on chart</th>{data.weeksDistribution.slice(0, 10).map((w) => <th>{w.weeks}</th>)}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Titles</td>{data.weeksDistribution.slice(0, 10).map((w) => <td class="tot">{w.n}</td>)}</tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The tail runs past this table — the longest run in the panel is{' '}
      {data.weeksDistribution[data.weeksDistribution.length - 1].weeks} weeks — but it is thin, and
      that thinness is the point. A chart run is not a duration most titles have.
    </p>
    {/*
      ⛔ 2026-08-07 신설. 빌드가 세어 둔 「걸러 낸 수」를 지면이 안 쓰고 있었다.
         세어 놓고 안 보여 주면 안 센 것과 같다. 수는 자료에서 읽는다.
    */}
    <p class="note">
      <b>What is not on this list.</b> Netflix files its global charts by a title’s primary
      language, and a Korean work belongs on the Non-English side. <b>{data.excludedEnglishChart}</b>{' '}
      titles matched a Korean work by name but sit on the English charts, so they are not that work
      and are out. <b>{data.excludedByHand.length}</b> more came out after we read them one at a
      time — {data.excludedByHand.slice(0, 3).join(', ')} among them. We hand-checked the largest
      {' '}{data.audited.tv} series and {data.audited.film} films by hours and stopped there; below
      that line the language rule is doing the work on its own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>All {data.shown}</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Title</th><th>Type</th><th>Weeks</th><th>Peak</th><th>Hours</th><th>Per week</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{r.title}</td>
              <td class="kind">{r.kind}</td>
              <td class="tot">{r.weeks}</td>
              <td>#{r.peak}</td>
              <td>{M(r.hours)}</td>
              <td>{M(r.perWeek)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/staying-power" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Netflix</b> — {data.source} Weekly global hours viewed across {data.weekCount} consecutive
        weeks, {data.weekFrom} to {data.weekTo}.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{data.shown} of {data.titleCount}.</b> Netflix publishes a top ten and nothing below it, and
        we hold week-by-week detail for the {data.shown} largest Korean titles. The other
        {' '}{data.titleCount - data.shown} are counted in the {bn(data.totalHours)}-hour total on
        {' '}<a href="/watched">the all-time page</a> but are not broken out here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Per week is arithmetic, not a Netflix figure.</b> It is total hours divided by weeks charted.
        A title that charted 4 weeks and one that charted 42 are not measured over comparable windows,
        and the column should be read as intensity while on the chart, nothing more.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Long series accumulate hours a film cannot.</b> Type is shown on every row for that reason.
        No adjustment is applied for runtime, because Netflix does not publish one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="back"><a href="/">&larr; Back to the front page</a></p>
  <KcwMethodLimits method={data.method} limitation={data.limitation} />
</WikiTip>

<style>
  h1 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; font-size: 1.65rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; line-height: 1.25; }
  .lead { margin: 0 0 2rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--muted); }
  section { margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 .7rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; line-height: 1.4; }
  h3 { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .05em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
       margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .note { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--muted); margin: .9rem 0 0; line-height: 1.6; }

  /* 두 목록을 나란히 — 겹치지 않는다는 것이 눈에 보여야 한다 */
  .two { display: grid; gap: 1.4rem; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 34rem) { .two { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.6rem; } }
  .two ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .two li { margin: .4rem 0; line-height: 1.4; }
  .nm { display: block; font-size: .9rem; }
  .meta { display: block; font-size: .76rem; color: var(--muted); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .84rem; }
  th, td { padding: .42rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: right;
           white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  th { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .03em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
       font-weight: 700; }
  th:first-child, td.nm { text-align: left; white-space: normal; }
  table td.nm { display: table-cell; font-size: .84rem; }
  td.kind { text-align: left; color: var(--muted); font-size: .78rem; }
  td.tot { font-weight: 700; }

  .src ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; }
  .src li { margin: .45rem 0; }
  .src b { color: var(--ink); }
  .back { margin-top: 2.5rem; font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
